(a) Priority of use; courts. The courtroom and adjoining rooms of the county courthouse and the county government complex are reserved for use in this order of priority:
(1) Superior court; state court of the county; probate court; and magistrate court. In case of conflict, the matter shall be resolved by the chief judge of the Northern Judicial Circuit, and in the event of his absence or disability, by the next superior court judge available and able to function in order of seniority of service;
(2) Activities related to the courts, such as Law Day activities, memorial observances, bar admission ceremonies, portrait dedications;
(3) Meetings of the board of commissioners, the board of education and other county, state or federal public boards, bodies, officials, representatives or senators, including the driver's license division of the department of public safety; when such space is needed for such meetings and is not otherwise readily available; provided, however, the courtroom shall not be used under this subsection by any official or anyone else for use in connection with any political campaign for any elective office;
(4) Birthday and other recognition ceremonies and observances for judges, district attorneys, assistant district attorneys, and other courthouse officials and employees; and
(5) All other purposes not prohibited by this section.
(b) Application. Use of the courtroom for the purpose set forth in subsection (a)(1) of this section shall be controlled by the superior court judges, and they shall also control activity uses set forth in subsection (a)(2) of this section, but as to the latter, notice shall be given to the office of the county administrator so that conflicts can be avoided. All other uses, set forth in subsections (a)(3) through (5) of this section, will be handled by the county administrator, and requests therefor must be submitted by written application on forms prepared and supplied by the county administrator showing:
(1) The name and address of the person, company or organization requesting use;
(2) If an organization, the names of the persons constituting the governing body or the persons in charge of the activities;
(3) The date, time, duration, and purpose of the use;
(4) The expected number of persons to be participating;
(5) The name, address and telephone number of the person authorized to act on behalf of the sponsoring organization or group;
(6) Any other information deemed appropriate by the county administrator.
(c) Usage fee. All private persons or groups desiring to use the courthouse courtroom and all purposes referred to in subsection (a)(5) of this section shall apply to the county administrator and complete an application form showing the name and address of the person, organization or group sponsoring the meeting; the purpose of the meeting; and the name, address and telephone number of the person or group applying for usage. The application must be accompanied by a fee as determined by the board of commissioners. The county administrator shall accept the applications and make the determination as to whether the person or group may use the courtroom and when, subject to the governing principles set forth in subsection (a)(5) of this section. The commissioners, for good cause shown, may waive the usage fee required above, on a fair and uniform basis.
(d) Time and action on applications. All applications required hereunder shall be filed with the county administrator at least five days before the date for which use is desired and shall be promptly acted upon by the county administrator or his designee, and notification shall be given to the applicant's designated representative as shown on the application form.
(e) Denial of application.
(1) Applications for Group 5 applicants may be denied by the county administrator in these cases:
a. Where the meeting or activities planned by the applicant reasonably contemplates substantially more persons than can safely be accommodated in the courtroom; or where the activities are such that they cannot safely be conducted in the courtroom; or where such activities are prohibited by the state fire marshal or other competent authority;
b. Where a prior use has been already granted for the date desired by the applicant, or where use is required by the courts or any Group 2 or 3 users;
c. Where the use is likely to cause damage or injury to the courtroom or persons therein;
d. When the applicant has already had use of the courtroom within a period of 30 days prior to the date of the current application;
e. When the applicant fails to pay the fee set by the county administrator, or has failed to pay for any prior damages to the courthouse caused by the applicant with respect to a prior application.
(2) Where any application is denied, or where the county administrator desires that the board of commissioners make the decision, the applicant may appeal to the board of commissioners or any committee designated by it, whose decision will be final.
(Code 1996, § 16-2; Ord. No. 1995-100-13, § 1—5, 5-12-1997)

